Description
-----------
This module introduces the Python Set data structure.

A set is an unordered, mutable collection of unique
elements. Unlike lists and tuples, sets do not allow
duplicate values and do not maintain element order.

Sets are useful for removing duplicate data, performing
mathematical set operations, and checking membership
efficiently.

Python supports several set operations such as union,
intersection, difference, and symmetric difference.

Learning Objectives
-------------------
1. Understand what a set is.
2. Learn how to create a set.
3. Add new elements.
4. Remove existing elements.
5. Perform common set operations.
6. Iterate through a set.
7. Display set information clearly.

Functions Used
--------------
print()             Display information.
len()               Return the number of elements.
add()               Add a new element.
remove()            Remove an element.
union()             Combine two sets.
intersection()      Find common elements.
difference()        Find different elements.

Expected Output
---------------
The program demonstrates how to create, modify,
iterate, and perform common mathematical operations
using Python sets.

Coding Style
------------
This module follows clean code principles and PEP 8
recommendations for readability, maintainability,
and professional software development.
=========================================================
"""


def display_header():
    """Display the program title."""

    print("=" * 70)
    print("Python Sets")
    print("=" * 70)


def display_set(title, data):
    """Display all elements in a set."""

    print()
    print(title)
    print("-" * 70)

    for index, item in enumerate(sorted(data), start=1):
        print(f"{index}. {item}")


def main():
    """Main entry point of the program."""

    display_header()

    # =====================================================
    # Create Sets
    # =====================================================

    backend_languages = {
        "Python",
        "Java",
        "Go",
        "PHP"
    }

    cloud_languages = {
        "Python",
        "Go",
        "Rust",
        "JavaScript"
    }

    # =====================================================
    # Add New Element
    # =====================================================

    backend_languages.add("C#")

    # =====================================================
    # Remove Element
    # =====================================================

    backend_languages.remove("PHP")

    # =====================================================
    # Set Operations
    # =====================================================

    all_languages = backend_languages.union(cloud_languages)

    common_languages = (
        backend_languages.intersection(cloud_languages)
    )

    unique_backend = (
        backend_languages.difference(cloud_languages)
    )

    # =====================================================
    # Display Results
    # =====================================================

    print(f"Backend Languages : {len(backend_languages)}")
    print(f"Cloud Languages   : {len(cloud_languages)}")

    display_set(
        "Backend Languages",
        backend_languages
    )

    display_set(
        "Cloud Languages",
        cloud_languages
    )

    display_set(
        "Union",
        all_languages
    )

    display_set(
        "Intersection",
        common_languages
    )

    display_set(
        "Difference",
        unique_backend
    )

    print()
    print("=" * 70)
    print("Program Completed Successfully")
    print("=" * 70)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
